WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.360
With the revival of "Night&nbsp;
Court" on screens everywhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.734
there are some characters who&nbsp;
unfortunately can't make a return.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.734 --> 00:00:11.696
From Harry Anderson to Markie Post,&nbsp;
here are several actors we've lost.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.360
Actor and magician Harry Anderson&nbsp;
played Judge Harry T. Stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.560
the goofy and affable star of "Night&nbsp;
Court." Appointed to the bench on a fluke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.540
his use of gags, magic tricks, and silly&nbsp;
jokes belied a genuine sense of empathy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.800
strong moral character. He loved old&nbsp;
Hollywood stars like Jean Harlow and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.420
Marilyn Monroe and had a well-documented&nbsp;
soft spot for his beloved Mel Tormé.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.400
Anderson's background was in magic, and he&nbsp;
downplayed his abilities as an actor. His many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.960
appearances on "Saturday Night Live" and later&nbsp;
playing a character on "Cheers" named "Harry the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.720
Hat" revealed a magician with a fast patter who&nbsp;
loved hustling marks with acidic comedic timing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.400
Those were the roles that earned him the chance to&nbsp;
have a show built around him, combined with show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.500
creator Reinhold Weege's fascination with the&nbsp;
weird world of the New York night court system.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.840
After "Night Court," Anderson's most memorable&nbsp;
role was as syndicated newspaper columnist Dave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.360
Barry on "Dave's World." He quit acting after that&nbsp;
and went back to magic, opening up a shop in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:17.090
Orleans and doing corporate gigs. He died in North&nbsp;
Carolina on April 16th, 2018, at the age of 65.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:22.260
The deadpan, nasal sarcasm of Selma Diamond was&nbsp;
a highlight of the first two seasons of "Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.000
Court." Diamond played bailiff Selma Hacker,&nbsp;
and she was part of an odd couple pairing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.920
Richard Moll's enormous Bull Shannon character.&nbsp;
While Bull looked fearsome, he was quite gentle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:36.734
However, while Selma looked harmless, her&nbsp;
cutting remarks reduced people to ribbons.

00:01:36.734 --> 00:01:40.154
"I had a husband just like that."

00:01:40.154 --> 00:01:40.932
"Oh yeah?"

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.460
"Yeah. I had to shoot him too."

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.680
Diamond got her start as a writer, working&nbsp;
with the likes of Mel Brooks on "The Milton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.180
Berle Show" and "Caesar's Hour." Diamond&nbsp;
appeared in several small roles in film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:59.100
and TV. A semi-recurring role in "Too Close for&nbsp;
Comfort" in the early '80s was the first time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:03.840
she had any serious traction as an actress.&nbsp;
Her role as Selma Hacker was a big breakout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:09.060
even if it didn't arrive until the age of 64.&nbsp;
She received an Emmy nomination as well as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.840
Golden Globe nomination for Outstanding&nbsp;
Supporting Actress In A Comedy Series.

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.140
Diamond died on May 13th, 1985.

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.180
Florence Halop replaced Selma Diamond as&nbsp;
bailiff for Season 3 of "Night Court," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:25.500
won over many fans of her own. Halop had&nbsp;
a television career that spanned nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.360
50 years. Her early break came while playing&nbsp;
Mama Bronson in the early '50s series "Meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.380
Millie." She spent decades doing guest&nbsp;
spots and voice work for cartoons like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.400
"Scooby-Doo" and "Captain Caveman,"&nbsp;
then became a valued utility player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.120
on "Barney Miller," where she played several&nbsp;
different characters during the show's run.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.780
As luck would have it, one of those&nbsp;
"Barney Miller" writers turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.920
to be "Night Court" creator Weege, and&nbsp;
he remembered her when he needed a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.880
bailiff. Before "Night Court," she was perhaps&nbsp;
best known for playing the patient from hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.880 --> 00:03:00.000
Mrs. Hufnagel, on "St. Elsewhere." Halop's Mrs.&nbsp;
Hufnagel was abusive, sarcastic, and racist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.580
then died of a nicked artery after heart surgery,&nbsp;
but the final image of her was darkly comedic:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.940
in a malfunctioning hospital&nbsp;
bed, "folded up like a clam."

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.152
Halop died on July 16, 1985, due to&nbsp;
complications from cancer. She was 63 years old.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.440
Actor and dancer Paula Kelly played Liz Williams,&nbsp;
the public defender in Season 1 of "Night Court."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.240
In a courtroom filled with eccentrics, Liz&nbsp;
represented a calming, serious presence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.620
the court. Caring and compassionate, Kelly's&nbsp;
Liz made an impression in her first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.420
being nominated for an Emmy in the category of&nbsp;
Outstanding Supporting Actress In A Comedy Series.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.180
Kelly had a long career on Broadway, originating&nbsp;
the role of Helene in "Sweet Charity." From there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.740
she switched from musicals and dancing&nbsp;
to drama, appearing in grim early '70s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.000
science-fiction classics like "Soylent&nbsp;
Green" and "The Andromeda Strain." Kelly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:52.260
then moved on to television, guest-starring&nbsp;
in several dramatic roles until appearing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:56.400
"Night Court." She followed up that show&nbsp;
with 24 appearances in the daytime soap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:59.640
opera "Santa Barbara" and eventually&nbsp;
picked up another Emmy nomination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.560
for her role in the 1989 Oprah-produced&nbsp;
miniseries "The Women of Brewster Place."

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.580
Kelly died on February 8th,&nbsp;
2020, at the age of 77.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.660
Legendary jazz singer Mel Tormé&nbsp;
was a favorite of Harry Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:17.280
as well as Harry T. Stone. With a photo&nbsp;
of "The Velvet Fog" adorning his chambers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:21.720
Stone's obsessive love of Tormé became&nbsp;
a running joke, which paid off when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.460
singer finally made an appearance on the show&nbsp;
in 1986, but Stone didn't get to meet him.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.680
In a later episode, Tormé played Herb, Stone's&nbsp;
guardian angel in an "It's A Wonderful Life"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.400
spoof where Harry was shown what would have&nbsp;
happened to everyone if he hadn't become a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.840
judge. In another classic episode called&nbsp;
"Melvin and Harold," Stone arranged for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:43.920
Tormé to play his birthday party, but&nbsp;
wound up sending him to jail instead.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.160
Some of Tormé's post "Night Court"&nbsp;
credits included an appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.660
Anderson's later show "Dave's World," as&nbsp;
well as an appearance on "Seinfeld." Tormé&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.140
was good-humored about his career, but&nbsp;
most of those appearances were genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.220
reverent toward his legacy. The composer&nbsp;
of such classics as "The Christmas Song"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:04.586
and "Jeepers Creepers" passed away&nbsp;
on June 5th, 1999, at the age of 73.

00:05:04.586 --> 00:05:09.840
Jack Riley was an instantly recognizable face&nbsp;
in multiple decades of television and film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.220
filling roles with dripping sarcasm&nbsp;
for more than 40 years. He played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:18.060
few different characters on "Night Court," but&nbsp;
perhaps the most memorable was in "Clip Show"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:24.060
as Beepo the Clown, a sullen defendant who held&nbsp;
Judge Stone's staff and a city auditor hostage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.540
causing them to reminisce about old&nbsp;
times while awaiting a resolution.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.820
Riley also played Dr. Flick, a sarcastic&nbsp;
surgeon who operated on Dan. The popular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.080
versatile character actor had become best&nbsp;
known for playing Bob Newhart's antisocial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.340
neurotic patient Elliot Carlin in&nbsp;
"The Bob Newhart Show." He later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.520
reprised the character on shows&nbsp;
like "St. Elsewhere" and "ALF."

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:48.480
In his later years, he populated such&nbsp;
beloved shows as "Punky Brewster,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.380
"Gimme a Break!" and "Diff'rent Strokes,"&nbsp;
then won over a new generation of fans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.700
as the voice of Stu Pickles on "The&nbsp;
Rugrats." The legendary Jack Riley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:03.120
a veteran of nearly 200 film and TV roles,&nbsp;
died on August 19, 2016, at the age of 80.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.400
"Night Court" included one of the biggest roles of&nbsp;
S. Marc Jordan's long career as a character actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.860
who appeared in Season 8 as Jack Griffin, the&nbsp;
blind cafeteria concessions man. Though his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:17.760
screen time was limited, Jack typically&nbsp;
got a good wisecrack in every episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.560
making him yet another colorful oddball in&nbsp;
an unorthodox workplace filled with them.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:29.100
"Two bucks for a pack of&nbsp;
stale licorice. I love guilt."

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:33.060
As time went by, fans began to anticipate&nbsp;
him as a welcome presence in each episode.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.420
Jordan got his start in theater,&nbsp;
appearing in many Broadway and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.320
off-Broadway productions. Jordan didn't&nbsp;
get his first television acting role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:44.580
however, until he was 44 years old and&nbsp;
didn't start working regularly until he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.780
was nearly 50 when he appeared on shows&nbsp;
like "Lou Grant" and "Hill Street Blues."

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.400
He worked steadily for nearly 30 years&nbsp;
after that, doing voice work, sitcoms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.000
dramas, and even video games. His&nbsp;
second biggest role besides "Night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:00.660
Court" was on the daytime soap opera&nbsp;
"Days of Our Lives," in which he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:08.594
38 appearances between 1986 and 1989. He&nbsp;
died on July 11, 2014, at the age of 83.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:13.560
Martin Garner was another character actor with a&nbsp;
familiar, friendly face who got a moment to shine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:18.240
on "Night Court." He played Bernie, the newsstand&nbsp;
vendor in the cafeteria who was attracted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.920
to Selma. She was totally uninterested in his&nbsp;
advances, but that didn't stop him from trying.

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.400
A classic character actor, he was&nbsp;
another person on "Night Court" with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.780
a theater background. However, he became best&nbsp;
known for his many television appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:37.080
starting in 1974 and continuing up through&nbsp;
1993. Sitcoms, dramas, action shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.760
and cutting-edge television such as "It's Garry&nbsp;
Shandling's Show" were all on Garner's resume,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:46.920
but perhaps his finest moment came when he&nbsp;
got to play Russian premier Mikhail Gorbachev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:53.871
on "The Super Mario Bros. Super Show!" Garner&nbsp;
died at the age of 74 on September 28th, 2001.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:58.380
Florence Stanley had a lot in common with&nbsp;
the actors who had a few appearances on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.380 --> 00:08:03.180
"Night Court." She had a career in television&nbsp;
and movies that spanned over 50 years. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.960
had worked with "Night Court" showrunner&nbsp;
Reinhold Weege before on "Barney Miller,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.760
playing the long-suffering Bernice Fish. She&nbsp;
continued that role in the spinoff "Fish."

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:15.840
At the same time she appeared on "Night&nbsp;
Court" as Judge Margaret W. Wilbur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:19.440
she played the same character as a&nbsp;
regular on the show "My Two Dads."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.940
Wilbur was the one who awarded custody of&nbsp;
a teenage girl to two single men who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:28.440
both been romantic interests for her mom&nbsp;
before she died. Wilbur also happened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:32.940
be their landlady. Richard Moll appeared on&nbsp;
the show as Bull Shannon, so Stanley returned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:37.560
the favor. The no-nonsense Judge Wilbur had&nbsp;
zero tolerance for Judge Stone's wackiness.

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:41.760
Stanley appeared in everything from "Dark&nbsp;
Shadows" to the video game "Atlantis:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:46.740
The Lost Empire." She died on&nbsp;
October 3, 2003, at the age of 79.

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:51.660
"Night Court" was a character actor's&nbsp;
paradise, and Phil Leeds proved that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:56.160
appearing five times in four different&nbsp;
roles. Perhaps the most poignant character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.960
Leeds played was a man who claimed to be God,&nbsp;
offering a frightened Harry surprisingly good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.980
advice after he received a death threat.&nbsp;
Leeds popped up again in another episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:09.120
where the same character had a disagreement&nbsp;
with another man who claimed to be God.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:13.260
Leeds was blacklisted during the&nbsp;
McCarthy era in the 1950s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:17.400
didn't work on television or film for&nbsp;
nearly nine years. Once he returned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:21.480
he made good use of his memorable looks.&nbsp;
He appeared in "The Patty Duke Show" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.520
"The Monkees" in the 1960s, and then played&nbsp;
the creepy Dr. Shand in "Rosemary's Baby."

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:29.940
Leeds was incredibly active up&nbsp;
until his death on August 16,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:35.280
1998. During "sweeps week" in May of that year,&nbsp;
Leeds appeared on no less than four different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:40.000
shows: "Ally McBeal," "Murphy Brown,"&nbsp;
"Ellen," and "Everybody Loves Raymond."

00:09:40.561 --> 00:09:45.120
Jason Bernard only appeared in two episodes of&nbsp;
"Night Court," but they were both very memorable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:50.640
As Judge Robert T. Willard, he demanded that new&nbsp;
judge Stone join their "fraternity of judges."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.100
Willard soon learned that Stone's methods weren't&nbsp;
exactly orthodox. He was so displeased by Stone in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.100 --> 00:10:01.140
general that he had him hauled before the Judicial&nbsp;
Review Board and tried to have him removed.

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:04.620
"Oh by the way, about that club&nbsp;
of yours? I don't really think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:07.980
I'm interested. I mean, you know,&nbsp;
no secret handshakes or anything."

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:13.800
Bernard had a serious and authoritative presence.&nbsp;
As a result, he often played authority figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:18.840
throughout his 30-year career. That included&nbsp;
judges, mayors, teachers, lawyers, doctors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.540
military officers, police officers, and ministers.&nbsp;
It also included films like "Liar, Liar" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:29.400
"Wargames," as well as a series of memorable&nbsp;
appearances on "Cagney and Lacey." Bernard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:35.100
died on October 16, 1996, of a heart attack.&nbsp;
He was 58 years old at the time of his death.

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.100
Throughout eight of the show's nine seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.840
Charlie Robinson added a steadying presence&nbsp;
as court clerk Macintosh "Mac" Robinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:47.340
whose easygoing demeanor and droll sense of&nbsp;
humor allowed him to own some of the funniest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:52.800
moments in the series simply by exuding an&nbsp;
aura of normalcy in a sea of colorful chaos.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:57.480
At the end of the show, Mac left to go to film&nbsp;
school, which is somewhat fitting, since Robinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.280
who'd already enjoyed a prolific career before&nbsp;
landing the role, continued working steadily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:07.080
in the decades that followed. Although much of&nbsp;
Robinson's resume includes single-episode stints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:12.060
on various television shows, some of his more&nbsp;
memorable roles include an appearance in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:17.760
1996 film "Set It Off" and a one-episode stop on&nbsp;
"How I Met Your Mother." Robinson's final screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:23.160
appearances came in the 2020 Freeform series&nbsp;
"Love in the Time of Corona." In July 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:28.653
he died at the age of 75 from cardiac&nbsp;
arrest and organ failure caused by cancer.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:32.940
If you were any kind of avid television&nbsp;
watcher in the '70s and '80s, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.940 --> 00:11:37.440
hard to escape Markie Post. The California&nbsp;
native landed a string of guest appearances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.640
on hit shows such as "The Love Boat" and&nbsp;
"The A-Team" before achieving her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:45.600
major TV breakthrough with the recurring&nbsp;
role of Terri Michaels on "The Fall Guy."

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:50.520
After Post's "Fall Guy" tenure ran its course,&nbsp;
she segued straight into playing public defender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:55.140
Christine Sullivan on "Night Court," a character&nbsp;
she played for the remainder of the series' run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:59.700
serving as half of a long-running love story&nbsp;
with Harry Anderson's Judge Harry Stone.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:04.260
After "Night Court" left the air in&nbsp;
1992, Post found her next series quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:09.180
co-starring in "Hearts Afire" opposite&nbsp;
John Ritter for three seasons. Post also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:12.420
acted in several films after "Night&nbsp;
Court," including playing the mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:16.980
of Cameron Diaz's character in the smash&nbsp;
1998 hit "There's Something About Mary."

00:12:16.980 --> 00:12:23.040
Post was diagnosed with cancer in 2017, and kept&nbsp;
working while undergoing treatment. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:28.778
those efforts proved unsuccessful; Post passed&nbsp;
away at the age of 70 in August of 2021.

